Archeologists:
Archeologists seem harder than they really are because they start out
very poorly equipped.  Specifically, the bullwhip is next to useless as
a weapon--use your pick-axe instead--and the fedora grants nothing to
your armor class, so you'll want to drop it as soon as you find a
non-cursed helmet.  Having a sack is very useful for protecting your
inventory from fire, theft, and (to some extent) water; it also enables
you to carry more than 52 different items.  Being able to identify gems
gives a good way to amass gold quickly--dig around the dungeon, find
gems, and sell them to shopkeepers--which is useful for buying early
protection.  Archeologists are also one of the few classes that can use
two weapons at the same time well; once armor is taken care of,
consider #twoweaponing.  (Lawful archeologists #twoweaponing
Grayswandir and a second silver saber do a *lot* of damage in
Gehennom!) Archeologists are also decent spellcasters, so don't neglect
your reading.

The quest has many snakes and mummies; if your weapon(s) is/are
blessed, you'll do slightly more damage to the mummies.  There are also
lots of traps, including a couple of mostly unavoidable rolling boulder
traps; when you can, smash the boulders to disable the traps.  The
filler levels are standard room-and-corridor.  As a bonus, there're
three(!) altars in the middle level of the quest, one of which will be
your alignment.  The Minion of Huhetotl hits fairly hard (two normal
attacks for 8-32 and 4-24 damage, plus a magic attack and a weak steal
attack).  He's also a demon, which means that hitting him with silver
weapons does extra damage.  Incidentally, there's a fourth altar at the
bottom of the quest.

The quest artifact is the Orb of Detection, which gives magic
resistance, telepathy, and half damage from spell attacks when carried;
#invoking it toggles invisibility.  Other than the half magic damage,
it's not much of an artifact--the magic resistance is nice, but don't
depend on it, in case the Orb gets stolen.  It's also a crystal ball,
and thus heavy.

Cavemen:
At first glance, cavemen are similar to barbarians, but with worse
stats and equipment.  At second glance, they still look that way.
Cavemen are intended for players who want a bit of challenge.  As such,
take advantage of anything the game gives you.  Rocks are always
plentiful, and you start out with a lot of them; don't let monsters
come up into club range without being hit on the head with a couple of
rocks, and you'll find combat becomes easier.

The quest is populated by bugbears, giants, dwarves, and potentially
mind flayers (maybe that's why cavemen tend to have low intelligence!).
 There's a free altar (actually, it's a guaranteed temple) on the home
level, which is very nice.  Annoyingly enough, there are several tight
squeezes to get through, which will limit how much you can carry into
the quest; one possibility is to through anything non-breakable through
the gap until you can fit.  The filler levels are caverns, like the
gnomish mines.  The quest nemesis is the Chromatic Dragon, who'll hit
you with random breath attacks if she's not in melee range; reflection
will help tremendously.  If she gets into melee range, be prepared for
her to hit hard--harder than all other dragons in the game.  On an
upside, she doesn't have many more hitpoints than other dragons.

The quest artifact is the Sceptre of Might, which grants magic
resistance while in your inventory; don't count on this, though, since
it may get stolen.  The Sceptre also does double damage against
monsters that don't share your starting alignment (no, you can't cheat
and wear a helm of opposite alignment), which can be useful if you
don't have a better weapon.  The best aspect, however, is that
#invoking the Sceptre toggles conflict on and off, so you can get
monsters fighting each other (rather than you!) without a ring; this is
very useful, especially late in the game.

Healers:
There are differing opinions on how to best play healers in the early
game, but the consensus seems to be that they're designed for a more
pacifistic approach.  Hide behind your pet; use the stethoscope to
check their health frequently (and check for hidden doors, which is
generally faster than searching), and heal them when necessary.  Work
your way down to minetown, and donate some of that starting gold pile
to the priest to raise your armor class--even if you're level 4 by the
time you get there, you should have enough money for one round of
protection.  Oh, and get a better weapon.  Everyone loves healers
(they've got high charisma), so shop prices will be low--never a bad
thing.  Experienced players will often play through the entire game as
a healer without attacking, though this takes a fair amount of skill,
patience, and discipline; it's not recommended for new players.
Healers also start with a quantity of healing potions, which if quaffed
immediately will boost your maximum hitpoints significantly; the stone
to flesh spell should help prevent starvation.

The quest has lots of rats, snakes, and a fair number of dragons.  It's
also very watery (with the requisite water monsters), so levitation or
magical breathing are recommended.  There's a neutral altar on the home
level, and a chaotic one halfway down.  The quest nemesis is the
Cyclops, and he hits hard--two hits for 4-32 points of damage each.
Even worse, he will almost definitely wield your quest artifact against
you, to potentially devestating effect.  Luckily, he's only resistant
to stoning--and healers just happen to start with a wand of sleep.

The quest artifact is the Staff of Aesculapius, and, as indicated
above, can be a very powervul weapon.  Specifically, it does double
damage against most monsters (everything except demons, undead, were
creatures, and Death), and periodically drains their levels, weakening
them further.  It also speeds up your hitpoint regeneration when
wielded, and can be #invoked to heal and cure various illnesses,
including getting slimed--so you don't have to set yourself on fire
like the other classes.  Just keep a good backup weapon in case the
staff gets stolen.
